            <ArticleTitle>Subacute angle-closure glaucoma as a cause of headache in the presence of a white eye.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>684-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Three patients, initially diagnosed with headache syndromes, were subsequently found to have subacute angle-closure glaucoma. In each case, the eye appeared grossly normal and there were no visual symptoms. A comprehensive ophthalmologic examination, including gonioscopy, confirmed the diagnosis. Laser iridotomy was curative with complete resolution of head and face pain. Subacute angle-closure glaucoma is an exception to the maxim that a white eye is not the cause of a painful eye.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
